You can use the `focus()` method to focus on an element found by `getElementsByTagName()`. Here's an example:
```
var elements = document.getElementsByTagName("input"); // get all input elements
var elementToFocus = elements[0]; // select the first element
elementToFocus.focus(); // focus on the element
```
This code gets all the input elements on the page using `getElementsByTagName()` and then selects the first one using bracket notation (`elements[0]`). Finally, it calls the `focus()` method on the selected element to focus on it.

Note that if you have multiple elements with the same tag name, `getElementsByTagName()` will return a HTMLCollection, which is an array-like object. You can then use bracket notation (`elements[0]`, `elements[1]`, etc.) to select the elements you want to focus on.

If you want to focus on a specific element based on its attributes or other conditions, you can use a loop to iterate through the elements and then focus on the one that matches your condition:
```
var elements = document.getElementsByTagName("input");
for (var i = 0; i < elements.length; i++) {
  var element = elements[i];
  if (element.id === "myInput") { // focus on the element with id="myInput"
    element.focus();
    break;
  }
}
```
